+Desert soil bacteria can persist on atmospheric hydrogen and carbon monoxide, which they oxidise with
+high-affinity enzymes. How the genes for these enzymes are distributed within and between desert
+soils is known mainly from single-time transects and from studies that treat hydrogen oxidation as one
+function. We typed the [NiFe]-hydrogenase and carbon monoxide dehydrogenase large subunits of 5{,}438
+species-level genomes from the hyperarid Rub' al-Khali and quantified each group as copies per genome
+equivalent in 546 shotgun metagenomes from 59 transect sites, sampled on up to five expeditions between
+2023 and 2025 at the surface, in bulk soil at 5--10~cm and beside plant roots at the same depth.
+Relative to bulk soil, root-adjacent soil carried 4.8-fold fewer group 1l hydrogenase copies per genome
+(106 paired site visits at 47 sites; lower in 89\%), 1.8 log$_2$ units more than the change in control
+hydrogenases and carbon monoxide dehydrogenases, whereas groups 1h and 2a and form-I carbon monoxide
+dehydrogenase changed no more than the controls. The relative depletion of group 1l scaled with
+extract DNA yield and arose almost entirely from turnover of genera, and genera carrying group 1l were
+depleted beside roots beyond what their predicted growth potential and phylum explained. Surface soil,
+in contrast, carried less of all four high-affinity groups than bulk soil. Across sites, group 1l in
+bulk soil was negatively associated with long-term precipitation, but this association was not robust
+to spatial blocking, whereas all four groups at the surface decreased with precipitation in every
+spatial sensitivity analysis. These patterns identify plant-associated relative exclusion of one
+hydrogen-scavenging guild as a replicated feature of hyperarid soil, which measurements of biomass,
+carbon and gas uptake can now test.
 
