 \section*{Introduction}
 Soil bacteria oxidise hydrogen and carbon monoxide at their trace atmospheric concentrations, and
-soil uptake is the largest sink of tropospheric hydrogen \citep{ehhalt2009h2cycle}. The oxidation
-is catalysed by enzymes with high substrate affinity: hydrogen by the group 1h and group 1l
-[NiFe]-hydrogenases \citep{greening2016genomic,ortiz2021antarctic,grinter2023structural} and carbon
-monoxide by the form-I carbon monoxide dehydrogenase \citep{cordero2019atmospheric}. Genomes
-encoding these enzymes are widespread in soil \citep{bay2021trace}, and the energy they yield
-sustains dormant cells \citep{greening2015persistence,cordero2019atmospheric}. In deserts, where
-photosynthetic input is sparse, atmospheric oxidation can also support primary production
-\citep{ji2017atmospheric,ray2022atmospheric}. The amount of this capacity in a soil therefore bears
-both on how desert communities persist and on the size of the soil hydrogen sink, to which arid
-regions contribute disproportionately \citep{reji2025h2sink}. What sets that amount within a desert
-is less clear.
-
-Three factors have been proposed, each from a different kind of study. Aridity gradients sampled
-once in space show that trace-gas oxidation gains weight as conditions dry: along a gradient in the
-Negev Desert, cell-specific rates of atmospheric hydrogen consumption increased 143-fold towards the
-hyperarid end together with the abundance of high-affinity hydrogenases \citep{bay2021aridity}, and
-in the Namib, hydrogen oxidation per 16S rRNA gene copy was highest in the most arid, fog-fed zone
-\citep{tribbia2026namib}. These transects compare places, and they treat spatial differences in
-aridity as a stand-in for change over time at one place, an assumption that sampling at a single
-time cannot test. Water addition experiments test moisture directly: hydrating an Australian desert
-soil in microcosms enriched cyanobacteria and decreased both the abundance and the expression of
-the group 1l hydrogenase associated with Actinobacteriota \citep{jordaan2020gobi}. These experiments
-follow one soil in the laboratory for weeks. Finally, plants concentrate organic carbon, and in a
-semiarid sandy desert the genes for hydrogen and carbon monoxide oxidation were less abundant under
-shrubs than in open soil and decreased as soil organic carbon increased, with the oxidisers behaving
-as oligotrophs whose uptake was suppressed by added carbon \citep{li2023fertile}. That study compared
-soil under and between shrubs over several depths and treated hydrogen oxidation as a single
-function, so it could not tell whether plants act on both high-affinity hydrogenase groups or on one
-of them. Most genome-resolved surveys of the high-affinity guild come from cold deserts
-\citep{ortiz2021antarctic,ray2022atmospheric,hutchinson2025antarctic}, and in situ data from hot
-deserts are among the gaps identified in a recent review \citep{ray2023mmbr}.
-
-We addressed these limitations in the Rub' al-Khali, a hyperarid sand desert in which long-term
-mean annual precipitation at our sites ranges from 14 to 48~mm. We sampled 60 transect sites on five
-expeditions between March 2023 and October 2025, each time at three soil positions: the surface, bulk
-soil at 5--10~cm and root-adjacent soil at the same 5--10~cm depth. Repeated visits allow spatial and
-temporal responses to precipitation to be compared directly, and the depth-matched root contrast
-separates the effect of plants from that of depth. To resolve the enzyme groups, we typed every
-hydrogenase and carbon monoxide dehydrogenase large subunit in a catalogue of 5{,}438 species-level
-genomes and quantified each group in the reads of 550 metagenomes as copies per genome equivalent,
-with non-high-affinity hydrogenases and carbon monoxide dehydrogenases as internal controls. With
-this design we tested three hypotheses: that root-adjacent soil selectively loses the group 1l
-hydrogen scavengers (H1); that hydrogen and carbon monoxide scavenging capacity respond separately
-to soil position (H2); and that long-term aridity sets the spatial level of group 1l capacity while
-change between expeditions at a site is independent of antecedent precipitation (H3). Because soil
-organic carbon was not measured, we then tested two proxies for the plant-carbon mechanism proposed
-by \citet{li2023fertile}: vegetation greenness and the predicted growth rate of the community.
-
-The analysis code, the compact inputs and outputs and the figure code are available at
-\url{https://github.com/bio-ontology-research-group/empty-quarter-mags} in the folder
-\texttt{lifeonair/}.
+soil uptake is the largest sink of tropospheric hydrogen \citep{ehhalt2009h2cycle}. Hydrogen is
+oxidised at atmospheric concentrations by group 1h and 1l [NiFe]-hydrogenases
+\citep{greening2016genomic,ortiz2021antarctic} and also by some group 2a enzymes
+\citep{islam2020group2a,grinter2023structural}; carbon monoxide is oxidised by the form-I carbon
+monoxide dehydrogenase \citep{king2007coox,cordero2019atmospheric}. Genomes encoding these enzymes are
+widespread in soil \citep{bay2021trace}, the energy they yield sustains dormant cells
+\citep{greening2015persistence,cordero2019atmospheric}, and in deserts atmospheric oxidation can
+contribute to primary production \citep{ji2017atmospheric,ray2022atmospheric}. Arid regions weigh
+heavily in estimates of the soil hydrogen sink \citep{reji2025h2sink}, yet how the capacity for
+trace-gas oxidation is distributed within a desert remains poorly resolved.
+
+Three factors have been examined, each with a different design. Along aridity gradients sampled once,
+the abundance of high-affinity hydrogenases and cell-specific hydrogen oxidation increased towards the
+dry end in the Negev \citep{bay2021aridity}, and hydrogen oxidation per 16S rRNA gene copy was highest in
+the most arid zone of the Namib \citep{tribbia2026namib}. Hydration experiments follow a single soil:
+wetting desert soil decreased the relative abundance and expression of group 1l genes while stimulating
+hydrogen uptake \citep{jordaan2020gobi}, and in another desert soil group 1l transcription increased
+during hydration \citep{imminger2024resuscitation}. Plants, finally, concentrate organic carbon. In the
+Mu Us Desert, genes for hydrogen and carbon monoxide oxidation were less abundant under shrubs than in
+open soil, decreased with organic carbon and behaved as traits of oligotrophs whose uptake was suppressed
+by added carbon \citep{li2023fertile}; most hydrogenases typed in that study belonged to groups 1d and
+2a. Across many plant species, predicted maximal growth rate is a leading predictor of which bacteria
+are enriched in the rhizosphere \citep{lopez2023rhizosphere}. Two questions remain open in hot sand
+deserts, which are under-represented among genome-resolved surveys
+\citep{ortiz2021antarctic,ray2022atmospheric,ray2023mmbr,hutchinson2025antarctic}: whether plants act on
+all high-affinity groups or on particular ones, and whether such a pattern reflects the growth strategy
+of the carriers, their phylogeny or the enzyme they carry.
+
+We addressed these questions in the Rub' al-Khali, a hyperarid sand desert, using shotgun metagenomes
+from 59 sites along a 1{,}043-km transect, sampled on up to five expeditions at three positions: the
+surface, bulk soil at 5--10~cm and root-adjacent soil at the same depth. The depth-matched contrast
+removes depth as a difference between root-adjacent and bulk soil, and repeated visits allow spatial
+and within-site associations with precipitation to be estimated separately. We typed every hydrogenase
+and carbon monoxide dehydrogenase large subunit in a catalogue of 5{,}438 species representatives and
+quantified each group in reads, using non-high-affinity hydrogenases and carbon monoxide dehydrogenases
+as controls that share the same normaliser. The analysis was exploratory. Our initial expectations, that
+plants would enrich hydrogen scavengers and that wetter years would reduce them, were not supported, and
+the questions below were formulated after inspecting the same data: whether the depletion of
+hydrogen-scavenging capacity beside roots is specific to one enzyme group; whether it reflects biomass
+scaling, lineage turnover or the growth strategy of the carriers; how the surface differs from bulk
+soil; and how robust the association with long-term aridity is across sites and over time. We report
+effect sizes with site-level uncertainty and control the false discovery rate within declared families
+of tests.
 
