 \section*{Results}
 
-\subsection*{Group 1l dominates hydrogen-scavenging capacity in Rub' al-Khali soil}
-We first established which high-affinity enzymes the Rub' al-Khali community encodes. Of the 5{,}438
-species representatives, 968 encode a high-affinity hydrogenase (597 group 1l, 410 group 1h, 39
-both), 1{,}170 a form-I CoxL and 1{,}860 at least one of them. The typed group 1l and 1h proteins
-form separate clades among the HydDB references (Fig.~\ref{fig:h123}a). They sit in hydrogenase
-loci: among loci with at least five genes on each side, a small-subunit gene is adjacent in 73\% of
-group 1l and 84\% of group 1h loci, and maturation genes are within ten genes in 88\% and 96\%; 73\%
-of form-I CoxL loci contain the complete \textit{coxMSL} operon. Group 1l proteins outnumber group 1h
-proteins 1{,}124 to 445.
-
-The same predominance holds in reads. In bulk soil, the median sample carried 1.08 group 1l copies
-per genome equivalent, 0.14 group 1h copies and 0.62 form-I CoxL copies. Group 1l is therefore the
-principal hydrogen-scavenging capacity, as in Antarctic and Gobi desert soils
-\citep{ortiz2021antarctic,jordaan2020gobi}, and carbon monoxide scavenging capacity is of comparable
-magnitude. With this baseline, the position contrasts below ask where in the soil each capacity is
-held.
-
-\subsection*{Root-adjacent soil selectively loses group 1l capacity}
-If plants select against hydrogen scavengers, root-adjacent soil should carry less high-affinity
-hydrogenase than bulk soil at the same depth, and the deficit should exceed any general change in
-gene content. We compared root-adjacent and bulk soil in the 107 site visits at 48 sites where both
-were sampled at 5--10~cm. Group 1l GE were lower beside roots by a median of 2.23 log$_2$ units, a
-4.7-fold difference (95\% CI $-2.82$ to $-1.57$; lower in 89\% of visits; $p=8\times10^{-9}$;
-Fig.~\ref{fig:h123}b). The control hydrogenases and CoxL were lower by 0.33 and 0.38 units, so the
-excess loss of group 1l beyond the controls was 1.97 units ($-2.53$ to $-1.05$;
-$p=3\times10^{-8}$). Group 1h was depleted no more than the controls ($-0.06$; $p=0.90$), and form-I CoxL was
-slightly better retained than the controls ($+0.17$, 0.08 to 0.23). In the mixed model, the
-interaction of marker and position was strong ($\chi^2=308$, 4 df, $p=2\times10^{-65}$), and the only
-interaction term that departed from the reference was that of group 1l ($-1.74$, SE 0.12).
-
-The loss was consistent across expeditions and lineages. Group 1l GE were lower beside roots in each
-of the five expeditions (median log$_2$ ratios $-1.6$ to $-3.4$), and all ten orders of group 1l
-carriers with the highest bulk-soil abundance were depleted, including Acidimicrobiales ($-2.8$),
-Solirubrobacterales ($-1.7$), Rubrobacterales ($-2.3$) and Gaiellales ($-2.4$)
-(Table~\ref{tab:lineages}). The excess loss was between $-1.61$ and $-1.97$ in every sensitivity
-variant: at 50 and 70\% identity, without contamination-flagged representatives, with weak group 1l
-calls removed, and without either the fifth or the fourth expedition (Table~\ref{tab:sensitivity}).
-Root-adjacent soil therefore selectively loses the group 1l hydrogen scavengers, as H1 predicted,
-while it retains group 1h and form-I CoxL capacity.
-
-\subsection*{Plant-carbon proxies explain a minor part of the group 1l loss}
-\citet{li2023fertile} attributed the depletion of trace-gas oxidisers under desert shrubs to organic
-carbon, which favours fast-growing copiotrophs over oligotrophic scavengers
-\citep{fierer2007copiotroph}. Without organic-carbon measurements, we tested two consequences of
-this mechanism. If plant input depresses group 1l, capacity should be lower where vegetation is
-greener, and the root contrast should shrink where bulk soil already receives plant input. Both held.
-Group 1l GE fell with NDVI in bulk soil ($-0.64$ log$_2$ units per standard deviation, SE 0.16) and
-at the surface ($-1.23$, SE 0.15), with mean annual precipitation in the model, with which site NDVI
-correlates ($\rho=0.57$); in root-adjacent soil, group 1l GE were unrelated to NDVI ($-0.02$). The
-excess loss beside roots was smaller at greener sites ($\rho=0.36$, $p=0.014$, 47 sites;
-Fig.~\ref{fig:mechanism}a).
-
-If organic carbon favours copiotrophs, root-adjacent communities should be faster-growing, and the
-group 1l loss should scale with that shift. We predicted minimum doubling times for 4{,}817
-representatives. Within orders, carriers of group 1l had longer doubling times than non-carriers
-(log doubling time $+0.12$, SE 0.03; medians 4.2 and 3.3~h), but so did carriers of group 1h
-($+0.11$, SE 0.03) and, to a lesser degree, form-I CoxL ($+0.06$, SE 0.02). The representatives
-with an estimate accounted for a median 98\% of mapped abundance. Root-adjacent communities were
-faster-growing than bulk soil in 79\% of 106 visits (community log doubling time $-0.25$;
-$p=1.5\times10^{-5}$; Fig.~\ref{fig:mechanism}b), and the group 1l excess loss was larger where this
-shift was larger ($\rho=0.22$, $p=0.023$; slope 1.55 log$_2$ units per unit log doubling time, SE
-0.62). The model, however, predicts an excess loss of 1.59 units in a visit with no shift
-(Fig.~\ref{fig:mechanism}c), close to the observed median of 1.97.
-
-Because carriers of both hydrogenase groups grow slowly, a shift to faster growers would deplete
-group 1h as well as group 1l. We therefore asked, across genera, whether carrying group 1l predicts depletion beside
-roots once growth rate is accounted for. Among 288 genera, slow-growing genera were more depleted
-(slope $-0.37$ per unit log doubling time, $p=6\times10^{-4}$), and the fraction of a genus carrying
-group 1l predicted additional depletion ($-0.75$ log$_2$ units, $p=3\times10^{-5}$) with phylum in
-the model; the fractions carrying group 1h ($+0.07$) and form-I CoxL ($-0.02$) were unrelated to
-depletion (Fig.~\ref{fig:mechanism}d). Plant input and the associated shift to faster growers contribute to
-the root-adjacent loss, but most of the loss is specific to group 1l and remains after growth rate
-and phylum are accounted for.
-
-\subsection*{Hydrogen and carbon monoxide scavenging capacity respond separately to soil position}
-The root contrast already shows hydrogen capacity falling while carbon monoxide capacity is
-retained. H2 tests whether this separation is general across positions and robust to the shared
-denominator. The raw-read ratio of high-affinity hydrogenase to form-I CoxL, which uses no
-normaliser, fell by 1.60 log$_2$ units from bulk to root-adjacent soil ($-1.94$ to $-1.15$) and by
-0.95 units from bulk to surface soil ($-1.45$ to $-0.48$). At the surface, group 1l GE were 1.03
-units and group 1h 0.62 units lower than in bulk soil, whereas form-I CoxL was unchanged ($-0.02$,
-$-0.09$ to 0.05; $p=0.13$; Fig.~\ref{fig:h123}c). Position explained 20.5\% of the variance in group
-1l GE, 5.5\% for group 1h, 3.1\% for form-I CoxL and 6--7\% for the controls.
-
-The two capacities also differ in how many lineages hold them. In the median sample, carbon monoxide
-capacity was spread over about 20 effective genera and high-affinity hydrogen capacity over 10 to 11,
-in every position. Carbon monoxide scavenging capacity is therefore held by more lineages and varies
-little with soil position, while hydrogen scavenging capacity, concentrated in group 1l carriers,
-tracks position, as H2 predicted.
-
-\subsection*{Long-term aridity sets the spatial level of group 1l capacity, while change over time is independent of recent rain}
-Position explains how capacity is distributed within a site. Between sites, H3 predicted that
-long-term aridity sets the level of group 1l capacity. Site medians of group 1l GE in bulk soil
-decreased with long-term mean annual precipitation ($\rho=-0.51$, $p=1.2\times10^{-4}$, 51 sites;
-Fig.~\ref{fig:h123}d), and with the independent NASA POWER product ($\rho=-0.43$, $p=0.0015$). The
-association held in a bootstrap over spatial blocks ($-0.81$ to $-0.18$), with each block left out
-($-0.61$ to $-0.37$), and after controlling for latitude, longitude and elevation ($-0.48$,
-$p=4\times10^{-4}$). Across all samples, group 1l GE fell by 0.49 log$_2$ units per standard
-deviation of log mean annual precipitation ($p=4\times10^{-5}$), group 1h by 0.14 ($p=0.09$) and
-form-I CoxL by 0.04 ($p=0.41$). The driest sites carry the most group 1l capacity, in line with the
-increase of high-affinity hydrogenases towards the dry end of other desert gradients
-\citep{bay2021aridity,tribbia2026namib}.
-
-Aridity also structures the community as a whole. The first principal coordinate of bulk-soil
-composition covaries with mean annual precipitation, and group 1l carriers form a large part of that
-axis; controlling for it reduced the correlation of group 1l GE with precipitation to $\rho=-0.20$
-($p=0.16$). The spatial association of group 1l capacity with aridity therefore operates through the
-composition of lineages, which changes along the aridity gradient.
-
-If spatial differences in aridity stand for change over time, group 1l capacity at a site should
-rise after dry years and fall after wet ones. Within sites, change in bulk-soil group 1l GE between
-expeditions was unrelated to the precipitation of the preceding year ($\rho=0.02$, $p=0.89$; 71
-visits at 27 sites). The confidence interval of the temporal slope ($-1.57$ to $1.70$ log$_2$ units
-per unit log precipitation) excludes the spatial slope ($-4.15$). At the surface, group 1l GE rose
-after wet years ($\rho=0.38$, $p=0.0017$), but antecedent precipitation differed mainly between
-expeditions, and with expedition in the model the association weakened to $p=0.31$.
-Form-I CoxL at the surface retained an association with antecedent precipitation after adjustment
-for expedition ($p=0.010$).
-
-The temporal result fits the stability of the communities over the 31 months of sampling.
-Expedition explained 2.0\% of the variation in community composition, compared with 29.4\% for site
-and 4.6\% for position, and the same partition held among carriers alone (2.0\%, 32.6\% and 3.7\%).
-Carrier genera fluctuated in step (median synchrony $\phi=0.115$ against 0.048 under the null at 37
-sites), so the stability of capacity reflects the persistence of the lineages that carry it. The spatial pattern therefore records long-term aridity
-through lineage composition, which persisted through the rainfall between our expeditions, and
-space-for-time substitution fails for group 1l capacity in bulk soil, as H3 predicted.
+\subsection*{Group 1l is the most abundant high-affinity hydrogenase in Rub' al-Khali soil}
+Of the 5{,}438 species representatives, 597 encode a group 1l, 410 a group 1h and
+134 a group 2a hydrogenase, and 245 a form-I CoxL (Fig.~\ref{fig:guilds}a,
+Fig.~\ref{fig:coxl}). In loci with at least five genes on each side, a small-subunit gene co-occurred
+with 73\% of group 1l, 84\% of group 1h and all group 2a large subunits, and maturation genes with 88\%,
+96\% and 100\%; the medium and small CoxL subunits co-occurred with 96\% of form-I CoxL. In bulk soil, the
+median sample carried 1.08 group 1l copies per genome equivalent, 0.14 group 1h, 0.03 group 2a and 0.11
+form-I CoxL. The read classifier was specific for these groups when the source genes were withheld. With
+all proteins sharing 50\% identity with the source removed from the database, 89\% of group 1l fragments
+were assigned to group 1l and 0.7\% to group 1h, 99\% of group 1h fragments to group 1h, 94\% of group 2a
+fragments to group 2a and 82\% of form-I CoxL fragments to form I; of 1.5 million fragments from
+non-marker genes, two were assigned to group 1l (Table~\ref{tab:benchmark}).
+
+\subsection*{Root-adjacent soil carries less group 1l relative to other genes}
+We compared root-adjacent and bulk soil in the 106 site visits at 47 sites where both were sampled at
+5--10~cm. Group 1l GE were lower beside roots by a median of 2.27 log$_2$ units, a 4.8-fold difference (95\%
+CI $-2.90$ to $-1.59$; lower in 89\% of visits; $p=3\times10^{-7}$; Fig.~\ref{fig:guilds}b). The control
+hydrogenases and CoxL were lower by 0.40 and 0.28 units, and the excess change of group 1l beyond the
+controls was $-1.83$ ($-2.62$ to $-1.00$; $q=2\times10^{-6}$). Groups 1h ($-0.01$), 2a ($-0.02$) and form-I
+CoxL ($-0.05$) changed no more than the controls (all $q>0.8$; Fig.~\ref{fig:guilds}d). In the mixed model,
+marker and position interacted ($\chi^2=324$, 5 df), and only the group 1l interaction departed from the
+reference ($-1.75$, SE 0.12; other high-affinity groups between $-0.08$ and $+0.07$). Position accounted for
+a gain in marginal $R^2$ of 0.22 for group 1l and 0.02--0.10 for the other markers.
+
+The difference was consistent across expeditions, analysis choices and lineages. The excess change of group
+1l was negative in each expedition ($-0.99$ to $-2.63$), between $-1.54$ and $-1.85$ in every sensitivity
+variant (Table~\ref{tab:sensitivity}), and $-1.82$ without a pseudocount and $-1.32$ with a pseudocount of
+0.1. All ten orders of group 1l carriers with the highest share of bulk soil had a lower share beside roots
+(Table~\ref{tab:lineages}).
+
+\subsection*{The depletion of group 1l scales with DNA yield and with genus turnover}
+Copies per genome equivalent fall either when carriers decline or when other organisms increase. Extract
+DNA concentration, a proxy that combines microbial biomass with extraction efficiency and plant DNA, was a
+median 10-fold higher in root-adjacent than in bulk soil (log$_2$ ratio 3.37, 47 visits). Across 267
+samples, group 1l GE fell by 0.32 log$_2$ units (SE 0.03) per doubling of DNA concentration, within visits
+($-0.30$) as well as between them ($-0.35$), within visits extracted with a single kit ($-0.36$) and within
+bulk soil alone ($-0.23$), whereas groups 1h and 2a ($-0.02$ and $-0.03$) and the controls did not scale with
+DNA (Fig.~\ref{fig:scaling}a). Under the scaling model this slope corresponds to an exponent of about 0.7:
+if DNA concentration tracked biomass, group 1l copies per gram would increase less than proportionally
+with biomass. Adjusting for DNA concentration reduced the root effect on group 1l from $-2.12$ (SE 0.23) to
+$-1.36$ (SE 0.20; Fig.~\ref{fig:scaling}c). Because DNA concentration is not a calibrated measure of
+microbial biomass, these data establish a relative depletion of group 1l and a sub-proportional scaling
+with DNA yield, not a loss of group 1l carriers per gram of soil.
+
+The change was carried by lineage composition. The share of the mapped community held by group 1l
+carriers fell from a median of 0.31 in bulk soil to 0.06 beside roots, and genus turnover
+accounted for 97\% of this change; the carrier fraction within genera changed little
+($-0.005$). The share of carriers fell by 2.17 log$_2$ units, their copy-weighted share by 2.30 and
+the number of copies per carrier by only 0.20, so the depletion reflects fewer carrier genomes rather than
+fewer copies in each.
+
+\subsection*{Genera carrying group 1l are depleted beyond their predicted growth potential}
+Root-adjacent communities had shorter predicted doubling times than bulk soil in 79\% of 106 visits
+(community-weighted log doubling time $-0.26$; $p=1.4\times10^{-5}$), consistent with the enrichment of
+fast-growing taxa in rhizospheres \citep{lopez2023rhizosphere}. Within orders, carriers of group 1l
+($+0.12$, SE 0.03), group 1h ($+0.12$) and group 2a ($+0.20$) had longer predicted
+doubling times than non-carriers, so a shift towards faster-growing organisms should deplete all three
+groups. Only group 1l, however, was depleted beyond the controls. The excess change of group 1l was larger
+where the community shifted more ($\rho=$0.26; slope 1.73, SE 0.61), but at zero shift
+the model still predicted an excess of $-1.57$ (site bootstrap $-2.22$ to $-0.93$;
+Fig.~\ref{fig:scaling}c). Across 289 genera, the fraction of a genus carrying group 1l predicted its
+depletion beside roots ($-0.81$ log$_2$ units, SE 0.18) with predicted doubling time, carriage
+of the other groups and phylum in the model, whereas carriage of groups 1h and 2a and form-I CoxL did not
+(Fig.~\ref{fig:scaling}b). The association held among genera with a nonzero ratio ($-0.92$) and among
+genera with at least two representatives ($-1.08$). Vegetation greenness was negatively associated
+with group 1l in bulk soil ($-0.80$ log$_2$ units per standard deviation of NDVI, SE 0.19) and at the surface
+($-1.17$, SE 0.16) with mean annual precipitation in the model, and the excess change beside roots was
+smaller at greener sites (site medians, $\rho=0.37$, 47 sites).
+
+Group 1l carriers also differed in genome traits. Within orders, they had higher GC content than
+non-carriers (+0.7 percentage points) and genomes of similar size, whereas group 1h, group 2a and
+form-I CoxL carriers had larger genomes (+7\%, +11\% and +17\%). Calvin--Benson--Bassham genes were
+encoded by 18\% of group 1l-only and 8\% of group 1h-only carriers, a difference that was not
+significant within orders (log-odds 0.73, SE 0.39, $p=0.06$) but was after adjustment for genome
+completeness (0.96, $p=0.02$).
+
+\subsection*{The surface carries less of all high-affinity groups than bulk soil}
+The surface contrast differed in kind from the root contrast. In 68 visits at 36 sites, all four
+high-affinity groups were lower at the surface than in bulk soil beyond the controls: group 1l by 0.83
+log$_2$ units, group 1h by 0.42, group 2a by 0.22 and form-I CoxL by 0.24 (all $q<0.02$;
+Fig.~\ref{fig:guilds}c,d). Root-adjacent soil therefore differed from the surface by a lower excess of group
+1l ($-0.85$) and a higher excess of group 1h ($+0.41$; 84 visits). At the surface, the excess change of
+group 1h increased with air temperature over the preceding 30 days (1.76 log$_2$ units per standard
+deviation, $q=0.001$), while associations with soil moisture and antecedent precipitation were not
+significant after correction.
+
+\subsection*{Association with long-term aridity is robust at the surface but not in bulk soil}
+Across sites, group 1l GE in bulk soil were negatively associated with ERA5 mean annual precipitation
+($\rho=-0.37$, $p=0.007$, 52 sites), with NASA POWER ($-0.42$) and with the blended product ($-0.53$), and
+residual spatial autocorrelation was weak (Moran's $I$ 0.03, $p=0.30$). The association held after
+controlling for latitude, longitude and elevation ($-0.33$) and for Ca, Cl and S ($-0.35$), but its spatial
+block bootstrap intervals included zero for every number of blocks (for six blocks $-0.65$ to $0.16$;
+Fig.~\ref{fig:scaling}d), and controlling for the first principal coordinate of community composition
+removed it ($0.01$). Groups 1h and 2a and form-I CoxL in bulk soil were not associated with precipitation
+after correction. At the surface, all four groups were negatively associated with precipitation
+($\rho$ between $-0.48$ and $-0.54$, all $q<0.002$, 46 sites), with block bootstrap intervals that excluded
+zero and with partial correlations that persisted after controlling for geography and geochemistry; residual
+spatial autocorrelation remained for group 1l (Moran's $I$ 0.16, $p<0.001$) but not for the other groups. In
+root-adjacent soil, only group 2a was associated ($\rho=-0.42$). Over all samples, a mixed model gave $-0.48$
+log$_2$ units per standard deviation of log precipitation for group 1l (SE 0.11) and between $-0.10$ and
+$-0.32$ for the other groups.
+
+The bulk-soil association, where present, again ran through lineage composition. Holding the carrier
+fraction of each genus at its mean reproduced the observed correlation of the carrier share with
+precipitation ($-0.37$ against $-0.38$), although within the 81 genera containing both
+carriers and non-carriers the carrier fraction also decreased with precipitation ($-0.29$ per standard
+deviation, SE 0.12).
+
+\subsection*{Within-site change between expeditions}
+Antecedent precipitation is now available for all five expeditions, covering 31 months. Within sites, after
+accounting for expedition, change in bulk-soil group 1l GE was not significantly associated with
+precipitation over the preceding year (103 visits at 35 sites; $q=0.34$) or 90 days, and the confidence
+intervals were too wide to exclude changes of 0.5 log$_2$ units across the within-site range of
+precipitation (Fig.~\ref{fig:temporal}). At the surface and beside roots, group 1l was lower after wetter
+90-day periods (changes of $-0.65$ and $-0.73$ log$_2$ units across the within-site interquartile range;
+$q=0.013$ and $0.020$), whereas changes in groups 1h and 2a and form-I CoxL in bulk and root-adjacent soil
+over 90 days were within the equivalence bound. Expedition
+explained 2.0\% of the variation in community composition with expedition permuted within site ($p=0.001$),
+site 28.8\% and position 4.6\%, and dispersion differed among expeditions ($p=0.012$). These data show that
+composition changed measurably between expeditions but do not determine whether group 1l in bulk soil
+tracks interannual rainfall.
 
 % ============================================================================
 \section*{Discussion}
-Plant roots and long-term aridity set the hydrogen-scavenging capacity of Rub' al-Khali soil through
-the group 1l [NiFe]-hydrogenase. Root-adjacent soil carried about a fifth of the group 1l capacity of bulk
-soil at the same depth while retaining group 1h and form-I CoxL capacity, and between sites, group 1l
-capacity was highest where long-term precipitation was lowest. Carbon monoxide scavenging capacity
-varied little with either factor and was held by about twice as many genera.
-
-The closest precedent is the loss of trace-gas oxidisers under shrubs in the Mu Us Desert
-\citep{li2023fertile}. Our results agree on the direction and on the involvement of plant carbon:
-greener sites carried less group 1l capacity, and root-adjacent communities were faster-growing.
-They add three findings. First, the loss is specific to one of the two high-affinity hydrogenase
-groups; resolving group 1l from group 1h shows that the plant effect falls on group 1l, even though
-carriers of both are slow-growing. Second, the loss is measured beyond control genes that share the same normaliser, which separates
-selection on the capacity from general changes in gene content. Third, plant-carbon proxies account for a minor part of the loss, and carriage
-of group 1l predicts depletion of a genus beyond its growth rate and phylum. A factor that
-distinguishes the group 1l carriers is therefore required. Moisture is the most direct candidate if
-root-adjacent soil is wetter than open sand: hydration of an Australian desert soil decreased the abundance and
-expression of group 1l while group 1h expression remained stable \citep{jordaan2020gobi}. Roots also
-alter the oxygen and hydrogen regime of the surrounding soil, and differences between the two enzymes
-in their tolerance of these conditions could favour group 1h beside roots. Both explanations remain to
-be tested.
-
-The separation of hydrogen from carbon monoxide capacity has a counterpart in Li et al., who found a
-weaker decline of carbon monoxide than of hydrogen oxidation genes under shrubs \citep{li2023fertile}.
-In the Rub' al-Khali, form-I CoxL capacity was retained beside roots and at the surface, and its
-distribution over more genera matches the functional redundancy reported for carbon monoxide
-dehydrogenase in southern African drylands \citep{saumasanchez2024redundancy}. A broad carrier base
-may buffer carbon monoxide scavenging capacity against the turnover of individual lineages, whereas
-hydrogen scavenging capacity, concentrated in fewer group 1l lineages, follows their distribution.
-
-The spatial association with aridity extends to a hot sand desert the pattern of other gradients, in
-which high-affinity hydrogenases and cell-specific hydrogen oxidation increase towards the arid end
-\citep{bay2021aridity,tribbia2026namib}. Repeated sampling adds a test over time. Change between
-expeditions at a site was unrelated to the preceding year's rainfall, and the spatial pattern
-operated through lineage composition, which varied about 15 times more between sites than between expeditions.
-Group 1l capacity therefore integrates aridity over periods longer than our 31 months of sampling.
-For models of the soil hydrogen sink, which weight arid regions heavily \citep{reji2025h2sink}, this
-implies that long-term aridity and vegetation cover predict where scavenging capacity is held, and
-that interannual rainfall variation changes it little.
-
-The design measures genomic capacity and its distribution. Capacity predicts uptake in some soils \citep{reji2025h2sink}, but activity depends on moisture and
-temperature \citep{jordaan2020gobi,hutchinson2025antarctic}, and the next step is to measure
-hydrogen and carbon monoxide uptake in archived root-adjacent and bulk soil from the same visits.
-Tribbia et al. measured uptake in 2~g of soil stored at 4$^\circ$C \citep{tribbia2026namib}, and
-potential rates in our frozen soil would test whether the root-adjacent loss of group 1l capacity
-reduces hydrogen uptake. Extracellular DNA can persist in soil \citep{carini2016relicdna,lennon2018relicdna},
-and only nine propidium-monoazide-treated pairs are available for this transect, so part of the
-measured capacity may be relic DNA, which treating the soil with propidium monoazide before
-extraction would remove. In the absence of soil organic carbon and moisture measurements, NDVI and
-predicted growth rate stand in for the plant-carbon mechanism; measurements of
-organic carbon and water content in the root-adjacent and bulk soil would test it directly. Finally,
-precipitation is taken from reanalysis at 0.25$^\circ$, and the five expeditions sample a limited
-range of antecedent rainfall, so a response to rare large rain events remains possible.
+Across 47 sites and five expeditions, root-adjacent soil carried a smaller share of group 1l hydrogenase
+genes than bulk soil at the same depth, and this was the only high-affinity group whose share fell more than
+that of control genes. The depletion scaled with extract DNA yield, was carried by turnover of genera rather
+than by changes within genera, and was associated with carrying group 1l beyond predicted growth potential
+and phylum. The surface showed a different pattern, with all high-affinity groups depleted, and only the
+surface pattern was robustly associated with long-term aridity.
+
+The closest precedent is the depletion of trace-gas oxidation genes under shrubs in the Mu Us Desert
+\citep{li2023fertile}. Li et al. measured organic carbon, carbon additions and uptake and attributed the
+depletion to oligotrophic oxidisers being outcompeted where carbon is available; most of their hydrogenases
+were of groups 1d and 2a. Our data add a group-resolved and replicated contrast: in the Rub' al-Khali, the
+plant-associated depletion was confined to group 1l, while groups 1h and 2a and form-I CoxL changed no more
+than control genes. Predicted growth potential is a dominant predictor of rhizosphere enrichment
+\citep{lopez2023rhizosphere}, and root-adjacent communities here were faster-growing, but carriers of groups
+1l, 1h and 2a all had long predicted doubling times, and only group 1l was depleted beyond the controls.
+Something that distinguishes group 1l carriers, or the habitats they occupy, is therefore needed to explain
+the pattern. Group 1l carriers are mostly Actinomycetota (59\%) and Chloroflexota (15\%) with high GC content, and the
+depletion was carried by whole genera, so the enzyme may mark a lineage ecology rather than be the target of
+selection. Moisture and oxygen are other candidates. Hydration decreased the relative abundance and
+transcription of group 1l in one desert soil \citep{jordaan2020gobi} but increased group 1l transcription in
+another \citep{imminger2024resuscitation}, and both studies found hydrogen uptake stimulated by water, so
+the direction of any moisture effect on group 1l carriers is unresolved.
+
+The scaling with DNA yield constrains what the depletion means. A lower share of group 1l genes beside
+roots is expected if root-adjacent soil supports more biomass of other organisms while group 1l carriers
+increase less than proportionally, and DNA yield explained about a third of the root effect. Relative and
+quantitative profiles of rhizosphere communities can differ in direction \citep{azarbad2022relative}, so
+measurements of cells or gene copies per gram are needed before the depletion can be called a loss of
+carriers or of capacity. The genus-level association and the absence of any comparable depletion of groups
+1h and 2a are not explained by a shared change in biomass, which would affect all groups alike.
+
+The surface differs from both subsurface positions. All high-affinity groups were depleted relative to
+bulk soil, and all decreased towards wetter sites, consistently across precipitation products and spatial
+sensitivity analyses. The surface is exposed to the largest fluctuations of temperature, radiation and
+moisture, and the association of group 1h with recent temperature suggests that the surface filters
+trace-gas oxidisers by exposure. In bulk soil, by contrast, the negative association of group 1l with
+long-term precipitation extends the pattern of other aridity gradients \citep{bay2021aridity,tribbia2026namib}
+only weakly: it did not survive spatial blocking, and it disappeared when community composition was
+controlled for. Mean annual precipitation varied only between 13 and 19~mm across our sites by ERA5, and
+reanalysis precipitation in this region is uncertain \citep{wang2025sara}, so the transect samples a narrow
+and imprecisely measured part of the aridity range. Over 31 months, composition shifted measurably between
+expeditions, and at the surface and beside roots group 1l was lower after wetter 90-day periods, but within
+bulk soil the data neither show nor exclude a response of group 1l to interannual rainfall.
+
+The design measures the relative representation of genes in DNA. It does not measure activity, uptake or
+abundance per gram. DNA concentration combines microbial and plant DNA and extraction efficiency, and a
+calibrated measure of biomass, such as quantitative PCR of the 16S rRNA gene or a spike-in, would test
+whether group 1l carriers decline in absolute terms. Extracellular DNA can persist in dry soil
+\citep{carini2016relicdna,lennon2018relicdna}; the propidium-monoazide-treated amplicon pairs available for
+this transect come from two sites and include no bulk soil, so the contribution of relic DNA to the position
+contrasts is not known. NDVI at 250~m and predicted growth potential are indirect proxies for the carbon
+supply of a root microsite, and organic carbon and water content of root-adjacent and bulk soil were not
+measured. Root-adjacent and bulk soil also differ in moisture, oxygen, plant identity and microsite, which
+the depth-matched design does not separate. Finally, the questions were formulated after inspecting these
+data. The root contrast is replicated across sites, expeditions and analysis choices, but potential hydrogen
+uptake in archived root-adjacent and bulk soil from the same visits, together with calibrated biomass, would
+provide the independent test.
 
